    "product_id": "55cb6ada-c365-459f-b5e3-d68dc3badffa",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 271150\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n450 AM PDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert through Friday. \n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region today \n  through Saturday, with gusty winds and heavy rain being the \n  greatest threat.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Early Next Week\n\nWell-above-normal temperatures are expected to remain across the \narea through Friday as high pressure remains centered near the Four \nCorners. Record highs were either broken or tied at Las Vegas, \nKingman, and Desert Rock on Wednesday, and it looks like another day \nof near-record heat before slight cooling on Friday-though \ntemperatures will still remain several degrees above normal. The one \ncaveat for temperatures today is the potential of seeing widespread \nmid- and high-level clouds, which could hold temperatures down a few \ndegrees. The Extreme Heat Warning will remain in place through \nFriday, but it is possible that we could catch a break if the clouds \nare thick enough. An approaching trough over the weekend will help \nshift the high east, lowering heights across the region. This will \nknock temperatures down closer to normal, with highs potentially \ndropping slightly below normal early next week. \n\nMonsoonal moisture will remain in place over the next couple of \ndays, leading to daily isolated to scattered afternoon and evening \nshowers and thunderstorms. Due to the position of the high, some \ntropical moisture will be pulled north, with PWATs increasing this \nafternoon as the 1.00 to 1.25 inch contour pushes northward up the \nColorado River Valley and into portions of Death Valley NP, while \nthe remainder of the area generally sees 0.75-1.00 inches. As we \nhave seen over the past few days, marginal instability will remain \nover the eastern half of the forecast area along with portions of \nsouthern San Bernardino County. An approaching trough will also \nbring stronger upper level winds from 700mb up to 300mb, resulting \nin better shear profiles for convective organization. Higher terrain \nwill see the best chances of storms, but any outflow boundaries \ncould help to generate new storms elsewhere. Any storm will have the \ncapability of producing heavy rain, flooding, strong winds, and \nlightning. Mid-range guidance does show drier air advecting into the \nregion Sunday into early next week, likely keeping monsoon activity \nat bay for a few days. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast \nPackage...Southwesterly winds will give way to light and variable \nwinds early this morning. Winds will pick up and settle from the \nsoutheast with 15 to 20 knot gusts later this morning, gradually \nveering around to the south by mid-afternoon. Isolated \nthunderstorms are expected to develop this afternoon with the \npotential of some storms impacting the terminal. Gusty erratic \nwinds are the main concern with storms, with lightning, brief \nheavy rain, and CIGs below 10kft also possible. Temperatures \nexceed 100F from 17Z-04Z today.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Typical wind patterns\nare expected through late morning with winds generally under \n10KT. South to southeast winds will increase in the early \nafternoon across much of the region with gusts 15-25KT expected. \nConvection will develop in Southern Nevada and Western Arizona, \nwith more widespread coverage and higher risk for impacts than the\npast few days. Gusty erratic winds, lightning, and CIGs below \n10kft are possible with any storms. Further northwest in Inyo \nCounty, mainly showers are expected. &&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28 \n              Record (Yr) Record(Yr) \n\nLas Vegas     110(2017)*  110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2019)*  103(2017)\nNeedles       120(1891)   116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(2017)   111(2017)\nKingman       107(2017)*  107(2009)\nDesert Rock   107(2017)   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  124(1924)   120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/CLIMATE...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
